\section{Cryogenic Thermal Shock Resistance Derivation}
\label{sec:cryogenic-shock-derivation}

\subsection{Problem Definition}
We consider a structural concrete specimen subjected to rapid thermal
cycling between cryogenic temperature
\[
T_{\text{cold}} \approx 77~\mathrm{K}
\]
and ambient/operational temperature
\[
T_{\text{hot}} \approx 300~\mathrm{K}.
\]
The objective is to derive the thermal stress state and failure
criteria governing shock resistance, and to show conditions under which
micro-fissuring and spalling are suppressed.

\subsection{Thermal Strain Field}
For a homogeneous, isotropic material with linear thermal expansion
coefficient $\alpha$, the free thermal strain induced by a temperature
change $\Delta T$ is:
\[
\epsilon_{\text{th}} = \alpha \Delta T,
\qquad
\Delta T = T_{\text{hot}} - T_{\text{cold}}.
\]
For the cryogenic cycle:
\[
\Delta T \approx 300~\mathrm{K} - 77~\mathrm{K}
= 223~\mathrm{K},
\]
yielding:
\[
\epsilon_{\text{th}} = \alpha \cdot 223.
\]

\subsection{Thermal Stress Under Constraint}
If the specimen is partially or fully constrained, the thermal strain
cannot fully develop, and an internal stress field arises. For
uniaxial constraint, the thermal stress is:
\[
\sigma_{\text{th}} = E \epsilon_{\text{eff}},
\]
where $E$ is Young's modulus and $\epsilon_{\text{eff}}$ is the
effective constrained strain. For full constraint:
\[
\epsilon_{\text{eff}} \approx \epsilon_{\text{th}}
= \alpha \Delta T,
\]
so:
\[
\sigma_{\text{th}} \approx E \alpha \Delta T.
\]

\subsection{Failure Criterion: Crack Initiation}
Crack initiation occurs when the thermally induced tensile stress
exceeds the material's tensile strength $f_t$:
\[
\sigma_{\text{th}} \ge f_t.
\]
Substituting:
\[
E \alpha \Delta T \ge f_t.
\]
Cryogenic shock resistance requires:
\[
E \alpha \Delta T < f_t.
\]

\subsection{Design Condition for Shock-Resistant Mixes}
Rearranging the inequality:
\[
\alpha < \frac{f_t}{E \Delta T}.
\]
Thus, for a given $\Delta T$ and modulus $E$, the thermal expansion
coefficient $\alpha$ must be sufficiently low, or the tensile strength
$f_t$ sufficiently high, to prevent cracking.

For cryogenic silica-dense aggregate concrete, both:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Reduced $\alpha$ (low thermal expansion),
    \item Enhanced $f_t$ (fiber reinforcement, dense microstructure),
\end{itemize}
contribute to satisfying:
\[
E \alpha \Delta T < f_t
\]
under $\Delta T \approx 223~\mathrm{K}$.

\subsection{Spalling Suppression}
Surface spalling is driven by steep thermal gradients and resulting
near-surface tensile stresses. The thermal gradient is:
\[
\frac{\partial T}{\partial x},
\]
and the corresponding stress can be approximated by:
\[
\sigma_{\text{grad}} \sim E \alpha \left( \frac{\partial T}{\partial x} \right) L,
\]
where $L$ is a characteristic depth. Shock-resistant behavior requires:
\[
\sigma_{\text{grad}} < f_t,
\]
which is achieved by:
\begin{itemize}
    \item High thermal conductivity (reducing $\partial T / \partial x$),
    \item High tensile strength $f_t$,
    \item Low $\alpha$.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Empirical Validation Link}
Ultrasonic pulse velocity measurements confirming zero micro-cracking
after repeated 77~K $\rightarrow$ 300~K cycles indicate that:
\[
E \alpha \Delta T < f_t
\quad \text{and} \quad
\sigma_{\text{grad}} < f_t
\]
are satisfied for the tested cryogenic silica-dense concrete mix,
thereby empirically validating the derived cryogenic shock resistance
conditions.